# cockpit-guac-rdp .envdefault - placed to [install path]/.env by install.sh (missing), reconciled on upgrade (new keys appended, your values never changed), then VALIDATED - a bad value refuses the install.
#
# THIS FILE REPLACES etcdefaults/edy-rdp. It is the same settings, in the same
# grammar, at a different destination - because it was `.envdefault` wearing the
# wrong hat. The rule (DEPLOY-CONTRACT section 5): does the software READ this to
# know how to behave, or does it ACT ON it as content? Behaviour is .envdefault.
# Second cut: can there be zero of them, or more than one? There is exactly one
# of these, always, and the relay does not start without it - so, .envdefault.
#
# Grammar (section 4.1) - a strict subset of what sh, Python and systemd's
# EnvironmentFile all accept:
#   KEY=value      KEY="value with spaces"      EMPTY=
#   full-line '#' comments only; a trailing '#' is PART OF THE VALUE
#   no $, no backticks, no interpolation
#
# Locations and settings only, NEVER a secret: install.sh and the relay's
# start-time bootstrap refuse a value that looks like a credential. The 3390 door
# credential is not here - it lives in grd's own credential store and is rotated
# by a timer.
#
# Every key below is validated (lib/edy-rdp-env.sh) when install.sh places or
# reconciles the .env, when --verify runs, and again by edy-rdp-bootstrap at every
# relay start: a value that does not validate names the key and the reason, and
# nothing starts on it (fail closed).
#
# Apply changes with:  systemctl restart edy-rdp-relay.service
# The units read this file directly (EnvironmentFile), so there is one copy of
# each setting on the host and not two.

# --- the relay --------------------------------------------------------------

# guacd endpoint the relay dials (host:port). guacd binds HOST LOOPBACK and is
# gated to the relay's uid by an nftables owner-match. Do NOT move it to a
# routable address: the owner-match, not network isolation, is what protects it.
EDY_RDP_GUACD=127.0.0.1:4822

# Unix group whose members are treated as Cockpit administrators for the
# server-side console gate. The elevation-proven token is still required; this
# only decides who may ask. Must exist on the host (getent group).
EDY_RDP_ADMIN_GROUP=sudo

# Where the relay persists its session registry. On tmpfs, so it does not survive
# a reboot - which is correct, because live guacd and grd sessions do not either.
# Absolute path.
EDY_RDP_STATE_FILE=/run/edy-rdp/state/sessions.json

# Relay and trace-logger verbosity: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR.
EDY_RDP_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- what guacd is allowed to dial (the SSRF controls) ----------------------

# LOCAL targets. Space-separated --allow-target flags, word-split into arguments
# by systemd. These are the grd backend ports on this host and nothing else.
# At least one --allow-target is required.
EDY_RDP_ALLOW_ARGS=--allow-target 127.0.0.1:3389 --allow-target 127.0.0.1:3390 --allow-target 127.0.0.1:3391

# REMOTE hosts, for the "Remote host" scenario (jump into another RDP host).
# A SEPARATE, FAIL-CLOSED allow-list: EMPTY MEANS THE FEATURE IS OFF. The user
# picks the target in the browser; the relay dials only what is listed here.
# Comma/space separated IPv4 or CIDR, each optionally :port (default 3389,
# :* = any port). The literal 'any' allows any host - understand what that means
# before you type it. IPv4 only. Examples:
#   EDY_RDP_REMOTE_ALLOW=192.168.2.0/24            that subnet, port 3389
#   EDY_RDP_REMOTE_ALLOW=192.168.2.20:3389,10.0.0.5:3390
#   EDY_RDP_REMOTE_ALLOW=any:*                     anywhere, any port: SSRF-open
EDY_RDP_REMOTE_ALLOW=

# 1 = the remote scenario additionally requires Cockpit administrative access.
# Empty, 0 or 1.
EDY_RDP_REMOTE_ADMIN_ONLY=0

# --- desktop-UI control (enable/disable/start/stop the graphical desktop) ----
#
# The "Desktop UI" panel can read this host's desktop state on any host (default
# target, display manager, sessions in use). WRITING that state -- turning the
# graphical desktop on or off, now or at boot -- is a SEPARATE, FAIL-CLOSED opt-in.
#
#   EDY_RDP_DESKUI_ENABLE=          (empty/0) status is readable, NO write verb runs
#   EDY_RDP_DESKUI_ENABLE=1         admins may enable/disable/start/stop the desktop
#
# DO NOT set this to 1 on a workstation whose graphical console you use: a "Stop"
# tears down the display manager and ends the local session. It is meant for hosts
# you administer remotely (servers, headless VMs) where the desktop is a managed
# service. Even when enabled, Stop and Disable require an administrator to type the
# host's name to confirm, and Stop refuses while a desktop session is in use unless
# that confirmation is given.
EDY_RDP_DESKUI_ENABLE=

# --- guacd ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pinned guacd container image, by DIGEST. Janua is guacd built against FreeRDP 3.
# The official docker.io/guacamole/guacd is FreeRDP 2, which lacks RDSTLS - the
# protocol grd's port 3390 greeter handover and the Remote-host scenario need - so
# with it the 3390 greeter and remote RDP break (KNOWN_ISSUES I26). requires.txt
# carries the same ref; deploy.sh --with-image pre-pulls it; install.sh --verify
# checks the RUNNING container is this image.
GUACD_IMAGE=ghcr.io/skylark-software/janua@sha256:2279eac000abbe78b89aa87f0a27912c1fa12c59f579a963ccbd4dc3804234e4
# Path to the guacd binary INSIDE the image above. Differs per image:
#   Janua (FreeRDP 3)        -> /usr/local/sbin/guacd      (default)
#   official guacamole/guacd -> /opt/guacamole/sbin/guacd  (FreeRDP 2: no 3390 greeter, no remote RDP)
GUACD_ENTRYPOINT=/usr/local/sbin/guacd

# --- audio streaming (optional, off by default) -----------------------------
# The Sound toggle streams the desktop's audio to the browser. guacd captures it
# from the seat's PipeWire/PulseAudio and the unit passes these vars into the
# container. How the socket reaches the container (docs/AUDIO.md, KNOWN_ISSUES I42):
# the host directory /run/edy-rdp-pulse is a SHARED mount, the seat's pulse socket
# is bound INTO it as 'native' by edy-rdp-pulse-bind (which refuses symlinks and a
# socket not owned by the seat uid: root mounts nothing it has not vetted), and the
# container mounts that directory read-only, as a slave, at /run/pulse - so guacd
# sees /run/pulse/native and can write nothing back. Because the directory is
# shared, a bind done at LOGIN (edy-rdp-pulse-seat@<uid>.path watches
# /run/user/<uid>/pulse and fires when 'native' appears) propagates live into the
# running container: audio works for the next session, no guacd restart. (A TCP
# endpoint was tried first but pipewire-pulse does not deliver recording audio
# over module-native-protocol-tcp.)
# To enable:
#   1. Find the monitor of the sink the desktop plays to, as the seat user:
#        pactl get-default-sink          # e.g. alsa_output.pci-0000_01_00.1.hdmi-stereo
#      then append ".monitor" for PULSE_SOURCE below.
#   2. Uncomment PULSE_SERVER + PULSE_SOURCE, then: systemctl restart edy-rdp-guacd
#
# Use an EXPLICIT monitor name, NOT the @DEFAULT_MONITOR@ alias: that alias does
# not resolve on pipewire-pulse and records silence. A sink's ".monitor" is what
# is PLAYING on the desktop -- never a microphone. Left unset = no audio channel.
#
# Numeric uid of the SEAT user whose pulse socket carries desktop audio. deploy.sh
# --with-units enables edy-rdp-pulse-seat@<this uid>.path, which binds
# /run/user/<uid>/pulse/native into the container's /run/pulse the moment it appears.
EDY_RDP_PULSE_SEAT_UID=1000
# Full path override of that seat socket (wins over the uid above when set).
#EDY_RDP_PULSE_SEAT_SOCKET=/run/user/1000/pulse/native
#PULSE_SERVER=unix:/run/pulse/native
#PULSE_SOURCE=alsa_output.pci-0000_01_00.1.hdmi-stereo.monitor
# Upgrading from 1.3.x: PULSE_SERVER=unix:/run/pulse.sock is refused as stale - change it to unix:/run/pulse/native.

# --- self-update (check for / apply / roll back a newer tagged Release) -----

# GitHub "owner/repo" this project's own Update tab checks for a newer tagged
# Release than what is installed. A location, not a secret. Checking and
# applying are available to any Cockpit administrator by default -- there is no
# separate enable flag for this feature, only this repo override.
EDY_RDP_UPDATE_REPO=x86Since8088/linux-cockpit-remote-desktop-guac

# --- the 3390 "Remote Login" door -------------------------------------------

# Username of the greeter door credential that edy-rdp-rotate-rdplogin rotates.
# This names the ACCOUNT, never the key: the key lives in grd's system
# credential store, and nothing writes it here.
EDY_RDP_DOOR_USER=rdplogin
